<Base title="Legacy" current="/archive" description="Shadow Hearts, a cult following and what came after Koudelka.">
  <div class="page ed">
    <p class="mono"><a href="/archive">The vault</a> · Legacy</p>
    <h1>Legacy</h1>
    <p>At release the reception was mixed: critics praised the atmosphere, the cut-scenes and the English cast, and complained about slow pacing, slow battles and weapons that wear out. Scores ran from the low sixties to the high sixties out of a hundred. No sales figures were ever published.</p>
    <p>Its afterlife is better than its reviews. The game gathered a devoted following for its mood and its connection to what came next.</p>

    <h2>Shadow Hearts</h2>
    <p>The sequel, <em>Shadow Hearts</em> (2001, PS2), is set in the same world after one specific ending of this game. Two more followed, in 2004 and 2005. Roger Bacon is the only character who appears in all four, and the Emigre Document is the thread that runs through them.</p>

    <div class="pics-grid pics-covers"><Plate slot="legacy-shadow-hearts-1" /><Plate slot="legacy-shadow-hearts-2" /><Plate slot="legacy-shadow-hearts-3" /></div>
    <h2>After</h2>
    <Plate slot="legacy-penny-blood" />
    <ul>
      <li>Matsuzo Machida, <em>Koudelka</em>'s art director, directed the <em>Shadow Hearts</em> series and later crowdfunded a spiritual successor, <em>Penny Blood</em> (2022).</li>
      <li>Hiroki Kikuta went on to score many other games and founded a label, Norstrilia. His own Bandcamp does not list the <em>Koudelka</em> soundtrack.</li>
      <li>In March 2025, after a translator praised the game’s writing and acting, Kikuta called it “a really happy surprise” that so many people are passionate about his work from thirty years ago (reported by Time Extension).</li>
      <li>The manga by Yūji Iwahara is set six months after the game. Kikuta saw it as a sequel at the time, but it is no longer considered canon.</li>
    </ul>

  </div>
</Base>
